  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Setting Integral Bounds from Overlap",
    "lesson_core_move": "Translate the overlap interval into the lower and upper $\\tau$ limits for the continuous-time convolution integral.",
    "section_count": 6,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Setting Integral Bounds from Overlap",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Setting Integral Bounds from Overlap",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "At this point in the convolution setup, the supports of $x(\\tau)$ and $h(t-\\tau)$ have already been intersected. The result of that intersection is the overlap interval: the set of $\\tau$ values where both factors can be nonzero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The integral only accumulates product values where $x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)$ can be nonzero. So once the overlap interval is known, the integral should be restricted to that interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The rule is direct: the left endpoint of the overlap interval becomes the lower limit of integration, and the right endpoint becomes the upper limit of integration.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{overlap: } a \\le \\tau \\le b \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y(t)=\\int_a^b x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This lesson is only about setting up the bounds. After the overlap has been translated into lower and upper $\\tau$ limits, evaluating the integral is a separate move.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Setting Integral Bounds from Overlap",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Setting Bounds from a Constant Overlap Interval",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given that the overlap interval is $1 \\le \\tau \\le 3$, set the bounds for the convolution integral $y(t)=\\int x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$ on that time region.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the given overlap interval $1 \\le \\tau \\le 3$. This already tells us where the product $x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)$ can be nonzero, so the task is only to turn those endpoints into integration bounds.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "1 \\le \\tau \\le 3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The left endpoint is $1$, so $1$ becomes the lower limit. The right endpoint is $3$, so $3$ becomes the upper limit.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The integrand stays the same because this step only changes the limits of integration. We do not evaluate the integral here.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_1^3 x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So on this time region, the correctly bounded convolution setup is $y(t)=\\int_1^3 x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Setting Integral Bounds from Overlap",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Setting Bounds with a $t$-Dependent Lower Limit",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "On a time region where the overlap interval is $t-2 \\le \\tau \\le 1$, set the bounds for the convolution integral $y(t)=\\int x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the overlap interval $t-2 \\le \\tau \\le 1$. Read it from left to right in the $\\tau$ variable: the expression on the left is the lower endpoint, and the expression on the right is the upper endpoint.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t-2 \\le \\tau \\le 1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Even though the lower endpoint contains $t$, it still becomes the lower limit. During the convolution integral, $t$ is held fixed while integration happens with respect to $\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $t-2$ goes below the integral sign, and the constant endpoint $1$ goes above the integral sign. The integrand stays $x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{t-2}^{1}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the completed bound setup for this time region. The step stops after placing the overlap endpoints as limits; it does not evaluate the integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Setting Integral Bounds from Overlap",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Setting Bounds with a $t$-Dependent Upper Limit",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "On a time region where the overlap interval is $0 \\le \\tau \\le t+1$, set the bounds for the convolution integral $y(t)=\\int x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the overlap interval $0 \\le \\tau \\le t+1$. Read the interval in the order of the $\\tau$ inequality: the left endpoint becomes the lower limit, and the right endpoint becomes the upper limit.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "0 \\le \\tau \\le t+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the lower endpoint is the constant $0$. The upper endpoint is $t+1$, and it stays in that upper-limit position even though it contains $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "During the convolution integral, integration is with respect to $\\tau$, so $t$ is held fixed. Place $0$ below the integral sign, place $t+1$ above it, and leave the integrand unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_0^{t+1}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the completed bound setup for this time region. The example stops at the correctly bounded convolution integral, before any evaluation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Setting Integral Bounds from Overlap",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Setting Bounds When Both Limits Depend on $t$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "On a time region where the overlap interval is $t-3 \\le \\tau \\le t+1$, set the bounds for the convolution integral $y(t)=\\int x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the overlap interval $t-3 \\le \\tau \\le t+1$. Both endpoints contain $t$, but the endpoint rule is unchanged: read the interval in increasing $\\tau$ order.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t-3 \\le \\tau \\le t+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The expression $t-3$ is the lower endpoint because it appears on the left side of the $\\tau$ inequality. The expression $t+1$ is the upper endpoint because it appears on the right side.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since the integral is with respect to $\\tau$, $t$ is held fixed while integrating. Keep both endpoint expressions in their positions, place them as the limits, and leave the integrand unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{t-3}^{t+1}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the correctly bounded convolution setup for this time region. Do not simplify the limits or evaluate the integral in this step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Setting Integral Bounds from Overlap",
        "section_index": 6,
        "section_title": "Writing Region-Specific Integrals from Overlap Bounds",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given the overlap intervals $0<t<1: 0\\le \\tau \\le t$ and $1\\le t<3: t-1\\le \\tau \\le 1$, write the region-specific convolution integrals for $y(t)=\\int x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the overlap information is already split by time region. Treat each time region as a separate case, and apply the same endpoint-to-bounds rule inside each case.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n0<t<1 &: 0\\le \\tau \\le t \\\\\n1\\le t<3 &: t-1\\le \\tau \\le 1\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the first region, $0<t<1$, the overlap interval is $0\\le \\tau \\le t$. The lower endpoint is $0$, and the upper endpoint is $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the second region, $1\\le t<3$, the overlap interval is $t-1\\le \\tau \\le 1$. The lower endpoint is $t-1$, and the upper endpoint is $1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now write one convolution integral for each time region. Keep the integrand the same in both cases and change only the limits.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n0<t<1 &: \\quad y(t)=\\int_0^t x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau \\\\\n1\\le t<3 &: \\quad y(t)=\\int_{t-1}^{1}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the region-specific setup. Do not merge the two regions or evaluate either integral in this step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
